diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is router6's RIP process configuration. The `router rip` block on router6 contains only `network 10.0.0.0/8`, which covers solely the r6–r3 WAN link (10.50.1.2/24 on eth2). The two Location C LAN interfaces — eth0 (172.18.50.254/24, connected to mswitch2 via r6_ms2) and eth1 (172.18.51.254/24, connected to mswitch2 via r6_ms2_2) — are absent from the RIP network statement. Because router6 never advertises 172.18.50.0/24 or 172.18.51.0/24, no router in the core carries a RIP route to Location C.

The mechanism producing the "flapping" symptom is as follows: with nothing to advertise (split-horizon suppresses all routes learned from router3 back toward router3), router6 sends no meaningful periodic RIP updates on eth2. FRR does not emit a purely empty response packet in this state, so router3 never gets a heartbeat from router6. Router3's "Last Update" counter for source 10.50.1.2 ages past 2 minutes (observed at 2:29) before an occasional triggered update arrives to briefly reset it (observed at 0:39), after which router6 disappears from router3's Routing Information Sources entirely — classic adjacency bounce. Network operators inspecting the RIP table on router3 see this as route flapping. Meanwhile, router4 and router5 have no route whatsoever to 172.18.x.x, and while r0–r3 carry manually-installed kernel static routes that partially patch connectivity along one path, these are fragile and entirely absent from the r4/r5 segment, producing the reported intermittent reachability across sites.

Root cause: the `router rip` configuration on router6 is missing `network 172.18.0.0/16` (covering both Location C subnets) and `version 2`.

Corrective fix: on router6, enter `vtysh`, go into `router rip`, and add `network 172.18.0.0/16` and `version 2`. This will cause router6 to originate RIP advertisements for 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24, establish a stable RIP adjacency with router3, propagate Location C routes to all routers via normal RIP convergence, and eliminate the perceived adjacency flapping.
=== END ===